<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurt Schuschnigg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kurt von Schuschnigg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kurt Schuschnigg was Chancellor of the Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1934 to 1938, succeeding the murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a>. He continued the authoritarian corporate state and tried to keep Austria independent of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, conceding much in the July Agreement of 1936. In March 1938 he called a referendum on independence but resigned under German threats, and he was imprisoned until 1945.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kurt Schuschnigg was born on 14 December 1897 in Reiff am Gartsee, now Riva del Garda, in the Tyrolean crown land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was taken prisoner on the Italian Front. He studied law at Freiburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, and after graduating in 1922 practised as a lawyer in Innsbruck.</p><p>He joined the right-wing Christian Social Party and in 1927 was elected to the Nationalrat as its youngest deputy. From January 1932 he was Minister of Justice, and from May 1933 also Minister of Education, under Karl Buresch and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a>. He openly discussed abolishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>, restored the death penalty, and after the socialist February Uprising of 1934 had eight insurgents executed at once.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Nazi Otto Planetta assassinated Dollfuss in the July Putsch, Schuschnigg became chancellor of the authoritarian Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 29 July 1934. He ruled mostly by decree and his policies differed little from those of his predecessor, though his rule was milder. He had to manage a near-bankrupt state while armed paramilitary forces owed loyalty to rival parties and the Austrian Nazis grew stronger. His overriding concern was to preserve Austria's independence.</p><p>He sought to balance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by aligning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, a policy that failed once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> sought Hitler's support. In July 1936 he signed an agreement with Germany that released imprisoned July Putsch insurgents and put the Nazi contact men Edmund Glaise-Horstenau and Guido Schmidt in the cabinet. On 12 February 1938 he met Hitler at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a>, where he was presented with what amounted to an ultimatum, including the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-seyss-inquart">Arthur Seyss-Inquart</a> as minister of security, and was coerced into agreeing.</p><p>On 24 February he told the Federal Diet that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> would go thus far and no further. He then proclaimed a plebiscite for 13 March. Hitler demanded its cancellation and then Schuschnigg's resignation. He resigned on 11 March, German troops entered Austria the next day, and he was placed under house arrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Schuschnigg was held in solitary confinement and then spent the war in Sachsenhausen and Dachau. In late April 1945 he narrowly escaped an execution order, was freed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> officers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and passed to American troops on 4 May 1945. He emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he was professor of political science at Saint Louis University from 1948 to 1967 and became a citizen in 1956.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party">Austrian People's Party</a> would not admit him, because it wished to distance itself from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. On returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> he downplayed his time of dictatorial power and tried to justify the regime. He died at Mutters, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, in 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kurt Schuschnigg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Kurt Schuschnigg</b> tried to preserve independence while yielding to German pressure under the 1936 July Agreement.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-austria">Federal State of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riva del Garda, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Mutters, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.88,10.85&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Riva del Garda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kurt Schuschnigg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5642 6142h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5642 6142h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.88,10.85&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
